 "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops",
 "category": "Advanced Graphs",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "description": "There are `n` cities labeled `0` to `n - 1`. Each `flights[i] = [from, to, price]` is a one-way flight. Return the cheapest price to get from `src` to `dst` with **at most `k` stops** in between, or `-1` if there is no such route.\n\n**Constraints**\n\n- `1 <= n <= 100`, `0 <= k < n`, `src != dst`",
 "function": "findCheapestPrice",
 "params": [
  "n",
  "flights",
  "src",
  "dst",
  "k"
 ],
 "starter": "class Solution:\n    def findCheapestPrice(self, n: int, flights: List[List[int]], src: int, dst: int, k: int) -> int:\n        pass\n",
 "hints": [
  "Plain Dijkstra ignores the limit on stops. A cheaper path might use too many stops.",
  "At most `k` stops means at most `k + 1` flights. Bellman-Ford relaxes paths one edge at a time, so `k + 1` rounds is exactly right.",
  "Run `k + 1` rounds of Bellman-Ford. In each round, update from a **copy** of the previous round's prices, so one round can't chain several flights together."
 ],
